# status/MULTIWORLD-PATCH.diff - the cumulative T103 + T108 WorldBridge / WorldEntry patch.
#
# Both files are human-owned, so Cowork never edits them; this is the proposal, generated
# against master as of 2026-09-20 (WorldBridge.cs 631 lines, WorldEntry.cs 313 lines) and
# verified to apply cleanly with BOTH `patch -p1` and `git apply -p1`.
#
#   cd D:\v100\TERA_SERVER.100\TeraSharp-cowork2
#   git apply status/MULTIWORLD-PATCH.diff        (or: patch -p1 < status\MULTIWORLD-PATCH.diff)
#
# It needs nothing else: World/WorldInstances.cs and World/WorldRegistration.cs (WorldRuntime,
# PerWorld, DefaultWorldId, WorldRouting, DungeonRouting) are already on the branch from T103
# and T108, and every symbol this patch names is public or internal there.
#
# WHAT IT DOES (status/MULTIWORLD-DESIGN.md sections 6 and 7)
#   T103  WorldLink gains WorldId / PlanetId / BypassIndex / Registered, filled from its own
#         SA_REGISTER (0x138A), which is now answered for real instead of replayed - today
#         every link on every World is handed world 0's CAPTURED reply.
#   T103  One TicketAllocator per World. A Ticket indexes THAT World's bypass slots, so two
#         Worlds hand out the same numbers and one global allocator collides.
#   T108  One WorldRuntime per World: IsReady, the game-id counter and the one-shot MarkReady
#         that DbProxy.OnWorldReady hangs off. Today world 13's handshake would zero world 0's
#         game-id counter under live players (design doc section 4 item 3).
#   T108  SendFrame(worldId, op, payload); the old two-argument form delegates to world 0.
#         LinksOf(worldId). A World's last link closing tears down only that World, and drops
#         its dungeon channels and in-flight transfers.
#   T108  The constructor fills WorldRouting's two hooks, which is what switches the already
#         committed dungeon routing in World/WorldInstances.cs from "always the arriving link"
#         to real cross-World routing. WorldEntry then targets AS_ENTER_WORLD at the World that
#         owns the instance.
#
# SINGLE-WORLD BEHAVIOUR IS UNCHANGED. An unregistered link reads as world 0; SendFrame(op, ..)
# resolves to the first world-0 link, which with one World is the old _links[0] primary; world
# 0's runtime starts not-ready with the counter at 0, so the first game id is still
# 0x80000AF00001; and both routing tables start empty. The one behaviour that DOES change on a
# single-World server is SA_REGISTER: the reply is built rather than replayed. All 25 captured
# pairs reproduce byte for byte (T103_the_world_registration_reply_is_byte_exact).
#
# APPLY IT AFTER THE DUNGEON CAPTURE (design doc section 5), not before - point 5 of that plan,
# whether world 0's and world 13's Tickets overlap, is the one question that could still change
# the shape of the tunnel map, and it is cheaper to answer before this lands than after.

